- Manufacturing Taste: simulation of canon formation (source: source.txt)
    Summary: Simulated cultural markets show patronage and feedback manufacture canons: quality filters but capital decides. (¶1, ¶2, ¶5, ¶45, ¶64)
        - 1 Canon question: we remember under 20 of thousands; patrons, not merit, may have chosen. (¶2, ¶3)
            - 1.1 Evidence: Vienna had 200 to 500 active composers; IMSLP catalogs over 3,400 survivors. (¶3)
            - 1.2 History cannot rerun; Haydn without Esterhazy patronage is empirically unanswerable. (¶4)
        - 2 Project tests three competing stories of canon formation. (¶6, ¶7)
            - 2.1 Story 1 Cream Rises: quality wins over time; replayed history yields same canon. (¶8)
            - 2.2 Story 2 Money Writes History: early exposure becomes familiarity, then canon; quality nearly irrelevant. (¶9)
            - 2.3 Story 3 Floors and Ceilings: extremes decided by quality, middle by luck and money. (¶10)
            - 2.4 Example: Job-market analogy: top and bottom rank easily; middle hires depend on luck. (¶11)
        - 3 Model combines three documented mechanisms to quantify their joint canon effect. (¶12, ¶13)
            - 3.1 Evidence: Mere exposure: liking grows with familiarity; Zajonc 1968, Bornstein 1989 r equals 0.26. (¶14) [reported]
            - 3.2 Nuance: Exposure advantage contaminates sincere judgment; money set the exposure. (¶15)
            - 3.3 Evidence: Social influence snowballs popularity; Salganik MusicLab 2006 ranked same song 1st or 40th. (¶16) [reported]
            - 3.4 Evidence: Cumulative advantage magnifies small starts; Merton Matthew Effect compounds court posts. (¶17) [reported]
            - 3.5 Open question: what happens when all three interact over generations. (¶18)
        - 4 Simulation uses producers, consumers, gatekeepers with independent quality and capital scores. (¶19, ¶20)
            - 4.1 Nuance: Quality and capital are uncorrelated; wealth does not make talent. (¶20)
            - 4.2 Capital buys exposure; consumers add quality, exposure, social signals; success feeds exposure. (¶21)
            - 4.3 Evidence: Thousands of runs; 1,000 producers, 10,000 consumers; 360 plus per condition with power analysis. (¶22)
        - 5 Results favor capital-plus-feedback over pure quality across experiments. (¶23)
            - 5.1 Evidence: Visible social influence significantly weakens quality-success link, p below 0.0001. (¶24, ¶25)
            - 5.2 Charts push us from independent judgment toward optimizing a popularity proxy. (¶26)
            - 5.3 Evidence: Talent held fixed across 200 runs; only patronage reshuffled. (¶27, ¶28)
            - 5.4 Evidence: Counterfactual distance 0.88; top-decile talent has 1-in-4 fame chance; middle is lottery. (¶29, ¶30)
            - 5.5 Without Esterhazy hire, Haydn likely joins forgotten hundreds despite excellence. (¶31)
            - 5.6 Evidence: Ablation: full 0.32, no-social 0.35, equal-capital 0.46, quality-only 0.46. (¶32, ¶33, ¶34)
            - 5.7 Equalizing resources raises quality link 44 percent; exclusion distorts most. (¶35)
            - 5.8 Modern gate: major labels reach playlists first; downstream is noise amplification. (¶36)
            - 5.9 Evidence: Stylized Vienna: 300 composers, distance 0.97, only 2.5 percent shared. (¶37, ¶38)
            - 5.10 Winners likely excellent, but dozens of equals lost the patronage lottery. (¶39)
            - 5.11 Manufacturing taste means losers were not meaningfully worse. (¶40)
            - 5.12 Evidence: Robustness plus-minus 50 percent: steep capital-to-exposure conversion erases quality fastest. (¶41, ¶42)
            - 5.13 Steep playlist reach gaps predict fame decoupled from quality. (¶43)
        - 6 Story 3 wins: quality sets floor and ceiling; capital and luck rule middle. (¶44, ¶45)
            - 6.1 Theme: Implications worth spelling out below. (¶46)
            - 6.2 Nuance: Bach greatest only among heard, published, preserved; equals may never have survived. (¶47)
            - 6.3 Feedback loops run faster today; playlists decide in hours, patronage took decades. (¶48)
            - 6.4 Leverage is wider exposure; blind auditions lifted women hires 5 to 25 percent. (¶49) [reported]
            - 6.5 Canon-as-best confuses metric with thing; curricula built on forgotten proxy. (¶50)
        - 7 Caveats bound what the model can and cannot tell us. (¶51, ¶52)
            - 7.1 Concession: Quality may be partly constituted socially, making decomposition ill-posed. (¶53)
            - 7.2 Concession: Calibration leaps from teens judging pop over weeks to aristocrats over decades. (¶54)
            - 7.3 Concession: Model omits genre, discourse, technology, politics; direction robust, magnitudes uncertain. (¶55)
        - 8 Bach was not Bieber, but closer than expected. (¶56, ¶57)
            - 8.1 Nuance: Bieber analogy gets mechanism right but quality floor wrong; court posts demanded skill. (¶58)
            - 8.2 Bach was excellent and well-positioned; positioning matters more than merit gap. (¶59)
            - 8.3 Example: Famous surgeon among fifty equals: profile led to talks, book, best reputation. (¶60)
            - 8.4 Leipzig job decided it; 20 to 50 equals forgotten for missing that post. (¶61)
            - 8.5 Canon is one of hundreds of valid possible canons, treated as inevitable. (¶62)
            - 8.6 Taste itself was manufactured: survivors set harmonic foundations we judge by. (¶63, ¶64)
            - 8.7 Future canons will reward distribution and algorithms over musical merit. (¶65)
